{
  "id": "002-spotlight-effect",
  "title": "Nobody noticed your embarrassing moment",
  "format": "short",
  "theme": "psychology",
  "voice": "af_heart",
  "speed": 1.0,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "That embarrassing moment you still replay at night? Most people never even noticed it.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "words",
        "words": [
          "nobody",
          "noticed"
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "noticed"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Psychologists at Cornell tested this.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "icon",
        "names": [
          "person"
        ],
        "label": "Gilovich et al., 2000"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "Cornell"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "They had students walk into a room wearing a cringey Barry Manilow T-shirt.",
      "visual": "same",
      "emphasis": [
        "Barry",
        "Manilow"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The students guessed that about half the room would notice.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "number",
        "value": 50,
        "label": "they guessed",
        "suffix": "%"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "half"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The real number? Only about one in four.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "compare",
        "title": "guess vs reality",
        "unit": "%",
        "left": {
          "label": "guessed",
          "value": 50
        },
        "right": {
          "label": "actually noticed",
          "value": 25
        }
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "four."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "It's called the spotlight effect.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "words",
        "words": [
          "spotlight",
          "effect"
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "spotlight"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "You live inside your own head all day, so every slip feels enormous.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "icon",
        "names": [
          "brain"
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "enormous."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "But everyone else is busy worrying about their own spotlight.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "people",
        "total": 20,
        "highlight": 5,
        "label": "actually watching you"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "their",
        "own"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "So that thing you're still embarrassed about? They've almost certainly forgotten it.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "question",
        "label": "who's really watching?"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "forgotten"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sources": [
    {
      "claim": "Students in an embarrassing Barry Manilow shirt guessed about 50% would notice; about 25% did (Gilovich, Medvec & Savitsky, 2000)",
      "url": "https://www.psychologytoday.com/ie/blog/are-we-born-racist/201206/the-spotlight-effect"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Spotlight effect: people overestimate how much others notice them",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spotlight_effect"
    }
  ],
  "publish": {
    "title": "Nobody Noticed Your Embarrassing Moment 🔦 (Science Proves It)",
    "description": "You think everyone saw it. Psychologists tested that, and the numbers say otherwise. This is the spotlight effect.",
